<i>- Everywhere we go</i>
<i>- Everywhere we go</i>

8
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,599
<i>- People wanna know</i>
<i>- People wanna know</i>

9
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:29,593
<i>- Who we are</i>
<i>- Who we are</i>

10
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:32,513
<i>- First platoon, Company B
<i>- First platoon, Company B

11
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:35,513
<i>- We're the best you'll ever see</i>
<i>- We're the best you'll ever see</i>

12
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,478
<i>- Company B Artillery</i>
<i>- Company B Artillery</i>

13
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:41,359
<i>- We're the best you'll ever see</i>
<i>- We're the best you'll ever see</i>

14
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,273
<i>To the left, to the left</i>
<i>To the left, right, left</i>

15
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:47,477
<i>- Everywhere we go</i>
<i>- Everywhere we go</i>

16
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:50,837
<i>- People wanna know</i>
<i>- People wanna know</i>

17
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,911
Keep up the pace.

18
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:55,640
<i>Left, left, left, right, left</i>

19
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:58,712
<i>One, two, three, four, left, right, left</i>

20
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:20,355
- Hear the news?
- I died?

21
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:23,308
Five perfect inspections in a row.

22
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:25,948
- Everybody's going in town tonight.
- Not me.

23
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:27,758
- You're not?
- No way.

24
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:32,911
In the last 24 hours,
I've cleaned two latrines,

25
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:34,559
policed the parade grounds,

26
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:39,874
run an obstacle course,
and painted the flag pole.

27
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:41,439
My back is killing me.

28
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:43,397
What used to be simple little blisters

29
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:46,996
on my hands and my feet
are now bleeding sores.

30
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:49,549
Now, as much as I hate
this little bunk,

31
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:52,598
when I lay my head on this pillow,

32
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:56,430
it will not move until morning.

33
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:01,435
Ten-hut!

34
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:05,954
Gentlemen, there will be
no night on the town tonight.

35
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:07,951
This platoon will be in full field gear

36
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:11,556
at 1 800 hours
ready for a little speed march.

37
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:13,429
Carry on.

38
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:23,038
Did you have any idea
that he was gonna run?

39
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:27,034
- No, of course I didn't.
- Where is he, Nancy?

40
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:29,714
Don't you think I'd tell you if I knew?

41
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:32,352
I swore I'd never get involved
with a soldier,

42
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:36,115
- and I never should have.
- You just can't fall out of love.

43
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:41,389
Hello?

44
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:44,599
Hi.

45
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:46,793
Yes.

46
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,433
Why?

47
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:51,558
No.

48
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:54,232
- Police.
- I've gotta go.

49
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:55,879
Yes, but--

50
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:58,198
All right.

51
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:06,238
Are you Nancy Lamont?

52
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:08,356
- Yes.
- We're looking for Private Hughes.

53
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:09,555
He's absent without leave.

54
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:11,073
- Do you know where he is?
- No.

55
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:13,117
- Have you heard from him?
- No.

56
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:15,032
A warrant's out for his arrest, ma'am.

57
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:17,589
Know what could happen
for aiding and abetting a fugitive?

58
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:19,318
I told you I don't know where he is.

59
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:21,471
- Check the bedroom.
- Just a minute.

60
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:24,518
- Do you have a search warrant.
- Mind your own business, miss.

61
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:26,830
- It's okay, Charlene.
- No, it's not okay.

62
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,877
Either you show me your warrant
or you get out.

63
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:30,632
Or you'll do what?

64
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:38,154
Get me the police. Emergency.

65
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:41,033
Let's go.

66
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:46,913
Thank you, Charlene.

67
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:48,513
Who was that on the phone?

68
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:51,717
- Just a friend.
- It was Chris, wasn't it?

69
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:54,874
Did he tell you where he is?

70
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:57,918
Nancy,
Chris is in very serious trouble.

71
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:00,594
- Not if they can't find him.
- Don't kid yourself.

72
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:03,957
You've simply got to tell me
where he is.

73
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:06,111
He'll ruin his life.

74
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:18,711
Hello, Chris.

75
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:27,679
- Nancy?
- Yeah.

76
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:29,796
I kind of halfway figured.

77
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:33,509
Well, she's a bright girl.
She cares for you.

78
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:37,719
She'd rather see you lead a full life
than an empty one or no life at all.

79
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:45,038
- I really screwed up this time, didn't I?
- You panicked.

80
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:50,428
Yeah. Yeah, I panicked.

81
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:51,874
Parker didn't show up.

82
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:54,315
I mean, let's face it,
I don't have a chance.

83
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:56,710
And I'll tell you something else.

84
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:59,312
I'm scared to death.

85
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:04,271
The old man was right.

86
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:06,670
I never will put it together.

87
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:12,469
Well, your father
wasn't always hot stuff.

88
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:16,797
He got his hands on a little money
and used it to make more money.

89
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:20,191
He was always good at that,
but he wasn't always hot stuff.

90
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:23,716
He had to kind of grow
into it like everybody else.

91
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:26,073
You know,

92
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:32,036
sometimes you look at a person
and you think that person

93
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:34,668
was always what you're looking at,

94
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:38,913
and that's not necessarily so.
I mean, just take me.

95
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,751
I wasn't always a lawyer.

96
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:44,388
I wasn't always successful.

97
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:49,277
I was once
just a little brown-headed kid,

98
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:52,275
and like all the other little kids
I knew,

99
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:57,594
I was just trying to do what I was
supposed to do and stay out of trouble.

100
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:01,631
Sometimes I'd miss, sometimes l--

101
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:03,877
Sometimes I'd miss pretty bad.

102
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:09,597
I spent two years
in the fourth grade.

103
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:14,475
You don't have to tell
that around too much.

104
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:46,589
Provide Private Hughes
with the appropriate government issue.

105
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:48,113
Then escort him to his cell.

106
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:50,510
I'll inform General Connors
he's been taken into custody.

107
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:52,875
That he turned himself in,
and inform your men

108
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:54,758
to keep their hands
and batons off him.

109
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:57,309
He'll be well taken care of, sir.

110
00:07:57,480 --> 00:08:01,951
Major, I'd like to bounce a couple
things off you. It'll just take a minute.

111
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:05,352
You know,

112
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:07,517
I saw in the coroner's report

113
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:11,355
that Lieutenant Walters
had a jagged cut across his hand,

114
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:13,431
one that could not have been caused
by a bayonet.

115
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:15,636
- Did you notice that?
- Yes, sir.

116
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:19,349
- How do you think he got it?
- I haven't the faintest idea.

117
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:23,911
Well, did you see anything in that room
that could have caused a cut like that?

118
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:25,069
No.

119
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:31,672
I think Private Hughes is being framed
for Lieutenant Walter's murder.

120
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:36,553
- That would take quite a lot of doing.
- Well,

121
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:39,314
take Private Hughes' bayonet,
for instance, the murder weapon.

122
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:42,438
Isn't it possible that somebody
took it out of his footlocker,

123
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:44,830
killed Lieutenant Walters,
and then put it back?

124
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:46,831
Possible, but unlikely.

125
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:48,877
Not in front of
a whole platoon of men.

126
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:50,837
Don't all bayonets look
pretty much the same?

127
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:51,989
Yes, sir.

128
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:54,276
Well, then someone could've switched
the bayonet

129
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:56,510
that was in Chris' footlocker
with the murder weapon,

130
00:08:56,680 --> 00:08:58,511
say at the time his footlocker
was searched.

131
00:08:58,880 --> 00:08:59,949
All that means

132
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:02,395
- is that it wasn't a frame.
- How is that?

133
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:04,915
Because I'm the one
who searched Private Hughes' locker.

134
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:08,356
Well, so much for theories.

135
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:12,753
Unless, of course we discover that you
had a motive to kill Lieutenant Walters.

136
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:15,753
- I didn't.
- Good. Good.

137
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:20,439
Because as I recall,
you don't have an alibi, either.

138
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:22,309
I'll see you, major.

139
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:48,273
Excuse me.

140
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:51,352
- I'm looking for Sergeant Parker.
- He's not here.

141
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:52,839
When do you expect him?

142
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:54,912
In about 30 days.

143
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:58,516
- He went on leave this morning.
- He went on leave?

144
00:09:58,680 --> 00:10:01,558
He's supposed to testify
in the Chris Hughes case.

145
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:04,837
He's my star witness.
Hell, he's my only witness.

146
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:07,676
- I'm sorry.
- You know where he went?

147
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:10,993
- No.
- I talked to Sergeant Parker yesterday

148
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:13,754
and he never said one damn word
about going anywhere.

149
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:17,833
Now, I wanna know who arranged this
leave and how it processed so quickly.

150
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:21,718
Listen, I don't have to answer
any of your questions.

151
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:23,233
I don't even know who you are.

152
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:26,233
My name's Ben Matlock,
and you'll answer my questions here

153
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:28,152
or in front of General Connors.

154
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:35,315
Well, Parker was due leave,
Parker asked for leave,

155
00:10:35,480 --> 00:10:37,914
and I gave Parker leave. Here.

156
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:40,355
I processed the paperwork myself.
Go ahead. Take a look.

157
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:45,471
Move it out!

158
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:57,751
All right, gentlemen,
through and back.

159
00:10:57,920 --> 00:11:00,354
The record is
two hours and 1 7 minutes.

160
00:11:00,520 --> 00:11:02,954
And there'll be weekend passes
if you break it.

161
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:04,709
Move them out.

162
00:11:04,880 --> 00:11:06,871
Left face!

163
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:09,998
Double time, march!

164
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:20,917
You'd think after Bobby died here
with Walters,

165
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:22,672
they'd pick something else
to torture us.

166
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:26,469
- This where it happened?
- Same place, same drill, everything.

167
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:30,151
- I twisted my ankle.
- Here, I'll give you a hand.

168
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:32,629
No, you go ahead. Go ahead.
I'll catch up.

169
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:52,278
Come on.

170
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:05,318
Wait a minute.

171
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:07,195
I thought I heard something.

172
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:18,198
Nothing.

173
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:26,397
Let's hurry it up.

174
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:38,917
Good night, ladies.

175
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:40,752
Evening ladies.

176
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:43,388
- Evening, Danny.
- Hey, Mr. Matlock.

177
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:44,993
Oh, mister, huh?

178
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:47,879
I think we know one another
well enough now. Call me Ben.

179
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:50,270
Okay, Ben.
I got another hot flash for you.

180
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:51,509
Yeah?

181
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:54,069
Poker game in the back room,
same guys,

182
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:56,276
just like nothing ever happened.

183
00:12:56,440 --> 00:13:00,433
Old habits are hard to break,
especially the bad ones.

184
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:03,034
Danny, is there anything
about what happened

185
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:05,634
that you didn't tell
the investigating officer,

186
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:07,677
anything that you or he
may have dismissed

187
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:11,389
as insignificant or immaterial?

188
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:14,035
No, I don't think so.

189
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:17,112
You mind going over it
one more time?

190
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:20,830
Well, about closing time,
the Hughes kid busts in here

191
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,549
looking like he just crawled out
of the woods, which, in fact, he had.

192
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:27,634
Anyway, I tried to stop him
from going in the back room,

193
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:29,392
but he was real determined.

194
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:32,950
I was cleaning up just like I am now,
so I thought, ''What the hell.

195
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:35,350
Who cares?''
I just wanted to go home.

196
00:13:35,520 --> 00:13:38,318
- Then what happened?
- Well, I heard a lot of yelling

197
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:41,030
and a bunch of scuffling
like a fight was going on.

198
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:43,714
Then everything got real quiet.

199
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:46,355
About a minute later, the kid left.

200
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:48,670
About two minutes later so did I.

201
00:13:48,840 --> 00:13:51,229
The men in the back room
just kept playing cards?

202
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:52,992
Yeah.

203
00:13:53,960 --> 00:13:55,075
What?

204
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:57,472
I do remember something.

205
00:13:57,640 --> 00:14:00,200
When I clean up, I like to scrub
the bar down real good,

206
00:14:00,360 --> 00:14:02,920
so I take off the ashtrays
and I stack them over there.

207
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:05,674
But when I came back to work
the day after the killing,

208
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:08,673
there was one ashtray right there.

209
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:11,032
- Anything in it?
- Yeah.

210
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:12,997
The butt of a cigar and some ashes.

211
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:15,358
Any of those guys smoke a cigar?

212
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:19,870
As far as I know, Captain Campbell,
sometimes Major Hamilton.

213
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:23,798
Hey, Danny, scotch on the rocks.

214
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:26,232
So much for getting out of here.

215
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:28,516
You go on home.
I'll tell them you left.

216
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:30,671
Hey, thanks, Ben.

217
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:34,272
Danny!

218
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:36,552
Danny's gone home.

219
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:41,195
Who's winning?

220
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:43,708
He is.

221
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:50,072
Four-handed poker.
Can't build up much of a pot.

222
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:53,038
Can't play high-Iow
or any split games.

223
00:14:53,800 --> 00:14:56,109
One of our regulars dropped out.

224
00:14:56,280 --> 00:14:58,396
You know,
one of the most puzzling things

225
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:01,438
about Lieutenant Walters' murder

226
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:05,233
is that little black book.

227
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:09,916
Why would the murderer bother
to take the record

228
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:14,915
of who owed what to whom
in a nice innocent poker game?

229
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:18,834
Who's keeping track
now that Lieutenant Walters is gone?

230
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:22,108
I am.

231
00:15:22,280 --> 00:15:25,192
What, you just zero everybody out
and start over?

232
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:28,029
We just took up where we left off.

233
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:30,794
We've got a pretty fair idea
of who owed what to whom.

234
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:34,592
I'm not much of a poker player.
I mean, once in a while.

235
00:15:34,760 --> 00:15:38,389
In games that I've seen you just
put your money down at the beginning

236
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:41,154
and pick up whatever's left
at the end.

237
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:43,231
I never heard of players
running tabs.

238
00:15:44,600 --> 00:15:46,113
We're friends.

239
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:48,514
I'm sure you are.

240
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:52,430
I just never heard of that.
Running tabs on a poker game.

241
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:56,354
It's strange. It's very strange.

242
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:15,756
- Hey, I've seen you in here before.
- That's right.

243
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:18,150
Come on over to the table.
I'll buy you a drink.

244
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:21,835
Well, that's very nice of you,
but not just now, okay?

245
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:23,194
You don't want a drink?

246
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:25,479
Thanks anyway.

247
00:16:35,320 --> 00:16:39,074
- Is this seat taken?
- Jim. No.

248
00:16:39,240 --> 00:16:40,958
Look, I'm sorry I'm late.

249
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:44,510
No, you're just in time.
I've been under attack.

250
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:46,399
Well, I can see why.

251
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:51,231
Here's to new friends.

252
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:53,038
Friends.

253
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:54,679
I thought you weren't thirsty.

254
00:16:54,840 --> 00:16:55,989
What's it to you?

255
00:16:56,160 --> 00:16:57,639
Come on.
My money's as good as his.

256
00:16:57,800 --> 00:17:00,155
- Take your hands off me.
- You heard her.

257
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:01,958
Just get in line, Jack.

258
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:03,553
Back off.

259
00:17:04,760 --> 00:17:06,478
No problem.

260
00:17:10,560 --> 00:17:12,676
- Okay.
- Okay. Hey, okay.

261
00:17:14,480 --> 00:17:16,118
I hope she's worth it.

262
00:17:20,840 --> 00:17:22,796
- You okay?
- Yeah.

263
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:26,989
That guy's got a hell of a right.

264
00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:29,951
Yeah, I'm okay. I think.

265
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:31,553
Good.

266
00:17:32,360 --> 00:17:36,148
- Look, I'm sorry about all that.
- No, no. It's not your fault.

267
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:41,511
I'll tell you what. There's a nice
little diner down the street.

268
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:45,798
The least I could do for my hero
is to buy him a cup of coffee.

269
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:50,752
I'll tell you my hard luck stories
and you can tell me yours.

270
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:53,437
Okay.

271
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:09,149
Good news.

272
00:18:09,320 --> 00:18:11,754
We went on field exercises last night
with Lieutenant Lynch.

273
00:18:11,920 --> 00:18:14,753
He took us to the same place
Walters used to take them.

274
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:18,356
So, when I heard that, I doubled back,
and you know what I saw?

275
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:21,990
I saw Lieutenant Lynch
and two civilians unloading packages

276
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:25,118
out of a four-wheeler
and hiding them in the truck.

277
00:18:25,680 --> 00:18:27,477
Now, when we were going
back to the post,

278
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:30,837
I heard Lynch say something about
he had to stop off at the supply depot

279
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,195
before taking the truck
back to the motor pool.

280
00:18:34,320 --> 00:18:37,039
I couldn't follow him.
I had to go on guard duty.

281
00:18:37,200 --> 00:18:40,954
Ben, this platoon sergeant
loves to hate me.

282
00:18:41,120 --> 00:18:44,157
- What was in the packages?
- I don't know.

283
00:18:44,960 --> 00:18:45,949
Well--

284
00:18:46,360 --> 00:18:50,592
All right, I'll find out, but Ben,
I have not slept in 36 hours.

285
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:52,997
I don't think we can wait.

286
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:54,959
All right.

287
00:18:55,400 --> 00:18:58,437
Supply depot tonight.

288
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:10,750
I'd like a rum
and whatever cola you've got.

289
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:14,879
- How are the tennis lessons going?
- I just want a drink, okay?

290
00:19:17,320 --> 00:19:19,993
Ms. Campbell,
I'm glad you could make it.

291
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:21,388
How have you been?

292
00:19:21,560 --> 00:19:24,518
Not so great, thanks to you.

293
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:30,398
I'm sorry, I really am.

294
00:19:30,560 --> 00:19:33,870
At the time I had no idea you'd
required so much medical attention.

295
00:19:34,040 --> 00:19:36,508
for broken bones and contusions

296
00:19:36,680 --> 00:19:39,911
I've never understood why a woman
stays with a man who beats her.

297
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:45,469
I've never understood why a woman
stays with a man who didn't.

298
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:49,312
You're a real piece of work,
Ms. Campbell,

299
00:19:49,480 --> 00:19:51,710
your husband abuses you,
you have an affair with a man

300
00:19:51,880 --> 00:19:54,519
that everybody
in the next ten miles despises.

301
00:19:54,680 --> 00:19:56,910
He was nice enough to me.

302
00:19:57,200 --> 00:20:00,590
Why did your husband and the others
play cards with him?

303
00:20:00,760 --> 00:20:02,113
I don't know.

304
00:20:02,280 --> 00:20:04,953
But did you have any other friends,
civilian friends?

305
00:20:05,120 --> 00:20:07,918
- He had no friends, period.
- Except you.

306
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:09,877
I guess I was enough.

307
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:12,836
And your husband
really didn't know?

308
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:14,911
What do you think?

309
00:20:15,080 --> 00:20:16,115
Paula.

310
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:22,590
Come on, let's go.

311
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:24,871
Would you please excuse me?

312
00:20:25,040 --> 00:20:27,838
It must be time
for my tongue lashing.

313
00:20:29,880 --> 00:20:30,915
Stay away from her.

314
00:21:01,440 --> 00:21:04,000
Colonel McRae
wants to ship this stuff.

315
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:06,835
And it's gotta go out tonight.

316
00:21:07,880 --> 00:21:08,869
Soldier.

317
00:21:09,760 --> 00:21:12,718
- Which one of you is Johnson?
- I am.

318
00:21:12,880 --> 00:21:14,711
I'm Lieutenant Hudson.

319
00:21:14,880 --> 00:21:16,757
Don't you salute a superior officer?

320
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:18,035
Yes sir.

321
00:21:18,560 --> 00:21:20,835
Sergeant wants to see you
at the motor pool on the double

322
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:22,592
and take your buddy with you.

323
00:21:22,760 --> 00:21:24,955
- But, sir--
- Soldier, I just gave you an order.

324
00:21:25,120 --> 00:21:27,270
Now, both of you, move it.
On the double.

325
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:52,628
You move, you're dead.

326
00:23:01,880 --> 00:23:04,030
We've got you on trespassing,
impersonating an officer,

327
00:23:04,200 --> 00:23:05,633
and destroying government property.

328
00:23:05,800 --> 00:23:06,789
It's all a mistake.

329
00:23:06,960 --> 00:23:10,316
- You'll say, ''sir,'' when you talk to me.
- It's all a mistake, sir.

330
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:12,152
Well, let's try it again.

331
00:23:12,320 --> 00:23:14,038
Why were you in the supply depot,
private?

332
00:23:14,200 --> 00:23:15,474
I was looking for drugs, sir.

333
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:17,198
What made you think
there were drugs?

334
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:18,918
I heard some talk in the mess hall.

335
00:23:19,080 --> 00:23:20,672
What kind of talking, private.

336
00:23:20,840 --> 00:23:22,910
Something about shipping cocaine.

337
00:23:23,080 --> 00:23:26,117
- Who was talking?
- I don't know. Just some guys, sir.

338
00:23:26,280 --> 00:23:28,316
So you took their word for it,
snuck into the depot,

339
00:23:28,480 --> 00:23:30,436
impersonated an officer,
started tearing boxes.

340
00:23:30,600 --> 00:23:32,272
Yes, sir.

341
00:23:32,840 --> 00:23:34,751
Do you think I'm an idiot, private?
Do you?

342
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:37,637
- No, sir.
- Think again.

343
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:41,870
Start all over again. Ten-hut.

344
00:23:42,040 --> 00:23:43,792
At ease.

345
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:45,792
I want this man released
immediately.

346
00:23:46,320 --> 00:23:48,788
We caught him trespassing
and destroying property, sir.

347
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:50,518
He's an associate of Mr. Matlock's.

348
00:23:50,680 --> 00:23:52,796
He was placed on this post
undercover.

349
00:23:52,960 --> 00:23:54,757
Why was I not informed of this, sir?

350
00:23:54,920 --> 00:23:56,319
You just were.

351
00:23:58,200 --> 00:24:00,395
For your information,
I wasn't impersonating an officer.

352
00:24:00,560 --> 00:24:03,313
I am an officer.
First Lieutenant Tyler Hudson,

353
00:24:03,480 --> 00:24:08,031
283243245,
United States Army Reserve.

354
00:24:20,120 --> 00:24:23,157
Ben, they're shipping cocaine
out of the supply depot.

355
00:24:23,320 --> 00:24:25,311
It's uncut. It's high-grade.

356
00:24:25,480 --> 00:24:27,550
I tasted it, I saw it, I can't prove it,

357
00:24:27,720 --> 00:24:31,076
and I can guarantee you whatever
was there, it's long gone by now.

358
00:24:31,240 --> 00:24:32,832
Damn.

359
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:35,077
How you doing with the hearing?

360
00:24:35,240 --> 00:24:36,992
Hearing's over.

361
00:24:37,160 --> 00:24:40,391
Court-martial starts tomorrow.
Does that answer your question?

362
00:24:52,160 --> 00:24:54,037
Major Hamilton,

363
00:24:54,200 --> 00:24:57,988
I show you the government's
Exhibit Number 4

364
00:24:58,160 --> 00:24:59,388
and ask if you recognise it.

365
00:25:02,160 --> 00:25:04,833
Yes, sir, I do.
That's my tag, my initials.

366
00:25:06,280 --> 00:25:10,512
Well, counsel for the defence
has already stipulated

367
00:25:11,360 --> 00:25:13,590
that this is the weapon
that killed Lieutenant Walters.

368
00:25:14,800 --> 00:25:17,872
Major, would you please tell the court
where it was found?

369
00:25:18,040 --> 00:25:20,156
- In Private Hughes' footlocker.
- Thank you.

370
00:25:20,320 --> 00:25:22,231
No further questions.

371
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:28,670
Who found this bayonet
in Private Hughes' footlocker, major?

372
00:25:28,840 --> 00:25:31,274
- I did.
- Have it checked for fingerprints?

373
00:25:31,440 --> 00:25:33,237
Yes, sir, none were found.

374
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:34,549
None on the blade?

375
00:25:34,720 --> 00:25:36,870
- That's right.
- How about the handle?

376
00:25:37,040 --> 00:25:38,189
None there either.

377
00:25:39,440 --> 00:25:41,795
Didn't that strike you as odd?

378
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:43,959
Didn't strike me at all, sir.

379
00:25:44,120 --> 00:25:48,352
I mean, why would Private Hughes
wipe his fingerprints off his bayonet

380
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:50,238
if he was gonna put it
in his footlocker?

381
00:25:50,400 --> 00:25:52,038
Objection. Calls for speculation.

382
00:25:52,200 --> 00:25:55,749
Let me put it this way. Doesn't
the fact that the bayonet was found

383
00:25:55,920 --> 00:25:58,593
in Private Hughes' footlocker
with no fingerprints

384
00:25:58,760 --> 00:26:01,638
suggest that someone
other than Private Hughes put it there?

385
00:26:01,800 --> 00:26:04,189
Objection. That's the same question.

386
00:26:04,880 --> 00:26:06,108
Sustained.

387
00:26:06,280 --> 00:26:08,032
No further questions.

388
00:26:15,120 --> 00:26:17,634
I call Sergeant Owens to the stand.

389
00:26:17,800 --> 00:26:20,030
Yes, sir. I work in the supply depot.

390
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:22,949
And where were you on the night
of January 1 1 ?

391
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:25,680
In the depot. I remember it
because there was a big shipment

392
00:26:25,840 --> 00:26:28,991
that was due out on the 1 2th and a lot
of paperwork that needed to be done.

393
00:26:29,160 --> 00:26:30,832
Just how late did you work?

394
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:32,911
Till about 2245.

395
00:26:33,080 --> 00:26:35,674
That's when Colonel McCrea
came in and relieved me.

396
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:40,269
You had to walk
past the officers' club

397
00:26:40,440 --> 00:26:42,271
to get to your car that night.
Is that correct?

398
00:26:42,440 --> 00:26:45,830
Yes, sir. And as I was passing by,
I heard this door slam.

399
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,958
That's what made me look up,
and I saw Private Hughes

400
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:51,270
come out of the building and--

401
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:55,513
- Well, he was running away.
- Thank you. No further questions.

402
00:27:03,920 --> 00:27:05,797
Sergeant Owens,

403
00:27:06,320 --> 00:27:09,915
you dropped out of school
to join the Army, is that correct?

404
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:11,989
Yes, sir.

405
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:16,793
When you send out a big shipment
like you did the night of the murder,

406
00:27:16,960 --> 00:27:18,871
what kind of paperwork's involved?

407
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:24,675
Transmittal letters and documents
have to be prepared,

408
00:27:25,240 --> 00:27:28,755
inventory's revised, telexes have to be
sent to bases receiving the materials,

409
00:27:28,920 --> 00:27:30,319
that sort of thing.

410
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:33,438
That sounds complicated.
You do it by yourself?

411
00:27:33,600 --> 00:27:36,319
Yes, sir. I'm just about the only one
on the base that can.

412
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:38,713
I think that's terrific.

413
00:27:39,960 --> 00:27:41,837
No further questions.

414
00:27:43,160 --> 00:27:45,754
- What was that all about?
- I think I'm on to something.

415
00:27:46,440 --> 00:27:48,476
Sir, the government rests.

416
00:27:49,040 --> 00:27:51,270
Is the defence ready, Mr. Matlock?

417
00:27:51,440 --> 00:27:53,908
We call Colonel McCrea
to the stand.

418
00:27:54,400 --> 00:27:58,313
You left the poker game
and went back to the supply depot

419
00:27:58,480 --> 00:28:01,119
to relieve your assistant,
Sergeant Owens. Is that correct?

420
00:28:01,280 --> 00:28:02,349
Yes, sir.

421
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:06,559
Was anybody in the supply depot
with you after Sergeant Owens left?

422
00:28:06,720 --> 00:28:08,631
No, sir. I was alone.

423
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:13,835
- Your Honour, at this time--
- Mr. Matlock--

424
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,594
Major Orlando, surely you can't
object to these papers

425
00:28:16,760 --> 00:28:18,796
before you even know
what they are.

426
00:28:18,960 --> 00:28:21,155
Mr. Matlock, in a military court,

427
00:28:21,320 --> 00:28:25,029
the judge is addressed as ''sir,''
not ''Your Honour.''

428
00:28:26,280 --> 00:28:27,395
Excuse me, sir.

429
00:28:28,600 --> 00:28:29,874
Sir,

430
00:28:30,040 --> 00:28:33,112
at this time, defence
would like to introduce into evidence

431
00:28:33,280 --> 00:28:37,796
these telex messages sent from
the supply depot here at Fort Simpson

432
00:28:37,960 --> 00:28:40,235
on the night of January 1 1th.

433
00:28:40,400 --> 00:28:44,791
Now, will you read the times
these telex messages were sent?

434
00:28:44,960 --> 00:28:46,632
According to the time codes,

435
00:28:46,800 --> 00:28:51,078
224 7, 2258, 2308 and 231 5.

436
00:28:51,240 --> 00:28:53,549
- Who sent them?
- It's not indicated.

437
00:28:54,720 --> 00:28:57,473
Well, you just told us
that you were in the supply depot alone

438
00:28:57,640 --> 00:28:59,517
at the times indicated
on these messages,

439
00:28:59,680 --> 00:29:02,274
so I guess that must mean
that you sent them.

440
00:29:03,240 --> 00:29:05,037
- I suppose so.
- Did you send them?

441
00:29:05,200 --> 00:29:06,838
Yes, I sent them.

442
00:29:09,840 --> 00:29:14,118
Defence Exhibit B
is a telex machine.

443
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:17,636
Actually, it is the telex machine
from the supply depot

444
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:19,711
that was used
to send those messages

445
00:29:19,880 --> 00:29:21,677
all set and ready to go.

446
00:29:21,840 --> 00:29:23,990
Now, colonel,
why don't you just come over here

447
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:25,912
and show us how you sent them.

448
00:29:26,080 --> 00:29:28,150
I just type them.

449
00:29:28,320 --> 00:29:30,595
It sure looks more complicated
than that to me.

450
00:29:30,760 --> 00:29:34,116
Don't be modest. Just step down,
come over here, and show us how.

451
00:29:48,640 --> 00:29:50,073
I can't.

452
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:53,152
I don't know how the telex works.

453
00:29:53,320 --> 00:29:56,869
Someone else must've sent
the messages. I don't remember.

454
00:29:57,040 --> 00:29:58,598
Yes, you do.

455
00:29:58,760 --> 00:30:01,320
Sergeant Owens
sent those messages, didn't he.

456
00:30:01,480 --> 00:30:03,232
No, no. He'd already gone.

457
00:30:03,400 --> 00:30:06,472
No, he hadn't. He didn't leave
the supply depot at 2245.

458
00:30:06,640 --> 00:30:08,631
He was there at least until 231 5.

459
00:30:08,800 --> 00:30:10,153
- Wasn't he?
- No.

460
00:30:10,320 --> 00:30:13,278
Which means he could not have seen
Private Hughes

461
00:30:13,440 --> 00:30:17,115
leave the officers' club
at the time of the murder, could he?

462
00:30:17,760 --> 00:30:20,957
- What's higher, a straight or a flush?
- A straight.

463
00:30:21,120 --> 00:30:22,348
Full house or four of a kind?

464
00:30:22,520 --> 00:30:24,192
Objection. Relevancy.

465
00:30:24,920 --> 00:30:28,151
Colonel McCrea has told us that
he was playing poker with the victim

466
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:30,436
less than an hour
before the murder.

467
00:30:30,600 --> 00:30:33,160
Now, Your Honour--
Excuse me, sir--

468
00:30:33,440 --> 00:30:37,069
That fact alone
makes this line of questioning relevant.

469
00:30:37,680 --> 00:30:40,035
- Overruled.
- Thank you.

470
00:30:40,200 --> 00:30:41,918
That is what you said,
isn't it, colonel?

471
00:30:42,080 --> 00:30:43,957
That you and three other men
were playing poker

472
00:30:44,120 --> 00:30:47,317
with Lieutenant Walters
in a back room of the officers' club?

473
00:30:47,480 --> 00:30:48,754
Yes.

474
00:30:48,920 --> 00:30:52,071
In a room with a wall mirror.

475
00:30:52,240 --> 00:30:53,832
A person playing poker at that table

476
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:58,676
could've seen the hands of one,
maybe two of his opponents.

477
00:30:59,240 --> 00:31:01,196
I gotta tell you something,
colonel.

478
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:04,716
That's some strange game.

479
00:31:05,480 --> 00:31:07,436
No further questions.

480
00:31:08,480 --> 00:31:10,516
No further question.

481
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:13,155
Colonel McCrea, you're dismissed.

482
00:31:16,480 --> 00:31:20,996
Sir, I'd like to request
a brief recess at this time

483
00:31:21,160 --> 00:31:24,277
for a consultation with an associate.

484
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:27,399
Ten minutes.

485
00:31:31,640 --> 00:31:33,710
I can't do it.

486
00:31:35,680 --> 00:31:38,240
- What do you mean you can't do it?
- Jim Lynch is my friend.

487
00:31:38,400 --> 00:31:40,709
Jim Lynch is a witness.
He's got to take the stand.

488
00:31:40,880 --> 00:31:42,791
I don't care
what your relationship is.

489
00:31:42,960 --> 00:31:45,155
- This is so hard.
- Charlene, you're a professional.

490
00:31:45,320 --> 00:31:48,232
I'm asking you
to keep faith with our client.

491
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:50,550
You know what profession
I feel like I'm in right now?

492
00:31:51,360 --> 00:31:54,591
All right, I'll question him,
but you've gotta be there for details.

493
00:31:54,760 --> 00:31:57,433
I'll do it.

494
00:31:58,560 --> 00:32:01,028
Charlene, I know how you feel,

495
00:32:01,360 --> 00:32:04,636
but as lawyers,
we've got to defend that boy.

496
00:32:08,320 --> 00:32:09,958
Is the defence ready?

497
00:32:10,600 --> 00:32:13,319
Sir, defence calls
Lieutenant Lynch to the stand

498
00:32:13,480 --> 00:32:16,836
and my associate, Charlene Matlock,
will question him.

499
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:19,833
Call Lieutenant Lynch.

500
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:21,718
Lieutenant Lynch.

501
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:47,437
Lieutenant Lynch,
you knew the victim, didn't you?

502
00:32:48,440 --> 00:32:49,839
Yes, I did.

503
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:52,355
In fact, you served together
during the invasion in Grenada.

504
00:32:52,520 --> 00:32:54,750
- Isn't that so?
- That's correct.

505
00:32:54,920 --> 00:32:57,673
Isn't it true that Lieutenant Walters
was blackmailing you

506
00:32:57,840 --> 00:32:59,831
for cowardice under fire?

507
00:33:00,440 --> 00:33:02,032
No. Absolutely not.

508
00:33:02,200 --> 00:33:04,555
And that he was forcing you
to participate against your will

509
00:33:04,720 --> 00:33:06,438
in certain criminal activities?

510
00:33:06,600 --> 00:33:08,318
That's ridiculous.

511
00:33:10,440 --> 00:33:12,032
Jim,

512
00:33:13,240 --> 00:33:16,198
I know that you confided in me
out of friendship

513
00:33:16,360 --> 00:33:19,079
and that you never expected the things
you told me to go any further.

514
00:33:19,240 --> 00:33:20,719
- I have no idea what--
- Jim,

515
00:33:20,880 --> 00:33:23,314
if you don't testify, my father's
gonna put me on the stand

516
00:33:23,480 --> 00:33:25,311
and force me to tell what you said.

517
00:33:37,560 --> 00:33:40,552
Did Lieutenant Walters

518
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:44,269
force you to participate
in certain criminal activities?

519
00:33:44,920 --> 00:33:46,114
Yes.

520
00:33:47,040 --> 00:33:49,998
Would you please tell the court
what these activities were?

521
00:33:51,800 --> 00:33:53,552
Lieutenant...

522
00:33:54,760 --> 00:33:57,115
Lieutenant Walters would purchase
large amounts of cocaine,

523
00:33:57,280 --> 00:33:58,918
which he then distributed

524
00:33:59,360 --> 00:34:02,079
through Army channels.

525
00:34:02,560 --> 00:34:04,630
Was anybody else involved?

526
00:34:04,800 --> 00:34:08,190
Colonel McCrea, Major Hamilton,
Captain Campbell,

527
00:34:08,360 --> 00:34:11,238
Sergeant Owens, two civilians,
among others.

528
00:34:11,400 --> 00:34:13,709
And just how
did the scheme work?

529
00:34:14,200 --> 00:34:16,873
Lieutenant Walters or I would
take a platoon out on manoeuvres,

530
00:34:17,040 --> 00:34:19,634
where we would secretly
meet with the two civilians.

531
00:34:19,800 --> 00:34:21,870
They would provide us
with the cocaine,

532
00:34:22,040 --> 00:34:25,191
which we would then take back
to the supply depot.

533
00:34:25,360 --> 00:34:27,669
And Colonel McCrea
would then make sure

534
00:34:27,840 --> 00:34:32,630
that it got to our contacts at posts
throughout the country.

535
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:34,950
And weren't the poker games
that you five took part in

536
00:34:35,120 --> 00:34:36,872
really just business meetings?

537
00:34:37,320 --> 00:34:38,912
A way of getting together
to place orders

538
00:34:39,080 --> 00:34:42,197
and exchange large sums of money
without arousing suspicion?

539
00:34:42,360 --> 00:34:44,032
- Yes.
- And wasn't the little black book

540
00:34:44,200 --> 00:34:46,395
that was taken
when Lieutenant Walters was killed

541
00:34:46,920 --> 00:34:49,593
actually a record
of these drug transactions?

542
00:34:49,760 --> 00:34:51,591
- Yes.
- Isn't that why he was murdered,

543
00:34:51,760 --> 00:34:53,159
because of the drug trafficking?

544
00:34:54,240 --> 00:34:56,879
I have no idea.
I left early on that evening,

545
00:34:57,040 --> 00:34:58,393
so I wouldn't know about that.

546
00:34:59,040 --> 00:35:00,712
Where did you go after you left?

547
00:35:01,400 --> 00:35:03,277
To a bar.

548
00:35:03,440 --> 00:35:06,398
I was there till about one.

549
00:35:06,560 --> 00:35:08,471
Thank you, Lieutenant.

550
00:35:11,320 --> 00:35:13,038
No further questions.

551
00:35:15,240 --> 00:35:17,629
Sergeant, place this man
under arrest.

552
00:35:17,800 --> 00:35:20,075
Colonel McCrea, Major Hamilton,

553
00:35:20,240 --> 00:35:22,515
Captain Campbell,
and Sergeant Owens

554
00:35:22,680 --> 00:35:24,557
are to be placed
in arrested quarters.

555
00:35:25,320 --> 00:35:28,357
This court is recessed until 0900
hours tomorrow.

556
00:35:29,360 --> 00:35:31,715
Well, what do you think?

557
00:35:32,240 --> 00:35:34,071
Nice piece of work.

558
00:35:45,760 --> 00:35:48,513
We're not close to acquittal yet,
are we?

559
00:35:48,680 --> 00:35:50,989
- Not yet.
- Not yet?

560
00:35:51,160 --> 00:35:52,639
Not yet.

561
00:35:53,840 --> 00:35:56,308
But there's an old rule
in the drug game--

562
00:35:56,480 --> 00:35:58,311
Follow the money.

563
00:36:10,880 --> 00:36:13,758
- My husband's not home.
- I know.

564
00:36:13,920 --> 00:36:17,037
I believe he's busy not answering
questions over at the post.

565
00:36:19,960 --> 00:36:21,473
I came to see you.

566
00:36:22,720 --> 00:36:24,472
Why don't you go in and watch TV?

567
00:36:24,640 --> 00:36:27,074
Mommy will be in,
in a few minutes, okay?

568
00:36:32,640 --> 00:36:34,676
Beautiful little girl.

569
00:36:35,200 --> 00:36:36,918
Yes, she is.

570
00:36:37,920 --> 00:36:39,433
Sometimes I think
she's the only reason

571
00:36:39,600 --> 00:36:41,716
I bother getting up in the morning.

572
00:36:41,880 --> 00:36:44,792
She's also the reason you dropped
the divorce, isn't she?

573
00:36:45,320 --> 00:36:46,719
What divorce?

574
00:36:46,880 --> 00:36:48,791
The one you filed two years ago.

575
00:36:48,960 --> 00:36:52,430
It may not be public knowledge,
but it is public record.

576
00:36:55,520 --> 00:36:57,795
Bill demanded custody.

577
00:36:57,960 --> 00:36:59,632
Swore he'd get it.

578
00:37:00,200 --> 00:37:02,589
Captain Campbell a wife beater?

579
00:37:03,200 --> 00:37:07,159
No one within 1 00 miles
would believe that, so I backed off.

580
00:37:07,320 --> 00:37:10,949
And you got even by having an affair
with a man he hated.

581
00:37:11,120 --> 00:37:12,997
That is where it started, isn't it?

582
00:37:14,200 --> 00:37:15,519
What do you mean?

583
00:37:15,680 --> 00:37:18,148
Mrs. Campbell, you visited
Lieutenant Walters' grave

584
00:37:18,320 --> 00:37:19,992
three or four times a week.

585
00:37:21,320 --> 00:37:24,676
That's not an act of vengeance.
That's an act of love.

586
00:37:29,080 --> 00:37:31,116
I really loved him.

587
00:37:36,640 --> 00:37:38,471
Some information
has come to my attention

588
00:37:38,640 --> 00:37:41,029
that leads me to believe that...

589
00:37:43,040 --> 00:37:44,917
your husband killed him.

590
00:37:50,960 --> 00:37:53,030
Hi, Jim.

591
00:37:53,840 --> 00:37:56,400
I hear you've been granted
immunity.

592
00:37:56,560 --> 00:37:58,869
Yeah. I've just given
the CID my statement.

593
00:37:59,040 --> 00:38:00,314
What happens now?

594
00:38:00,480 --> 00:38:02,436
Dishonourable discharge.

595
00:38:02,600 --> 00:38:04,591
I'm sorry.

596
00:38:05,800 --> 00:38:07,836
Actually, it feels kind of good.

597
00:38:16,880 --> 00:38:19,678
Don't tell me where you've been.
Just tell me you got the bank records.

598
00:38:19,840 --> 00:38:21,273
I got them.

599
00:38:23,640 --> 00:38:25,278
This court will come to order.

600
00:38:25,440 --> 00:38:27,556
Mr. Matlock, next witness, please.

601
00:38:27,720 --> 00:38:30,757
Sir, we call Captain William Campbell
to the stand.

602
00:38:30,920 --> 00:38:34,117
I understand that you
and Major Hamilton

603
00:38:34,280 --> 00:38:37,590
and Lieutenants Lynch and Walters
and Colonel McCrea

604
00:38:37,760 --> 00:38:40,069
were all in a back room
of the officers' club

605
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:43,312
on the night Lieutenant Walters
was murdered. Is that correct?

606
00:38:43,480 --> 00:38:46,870
- Yes, sir.
- And I understand that all of you

607
00:38:47,040 --> 00:38:50,430
were engaged in a conspiracy
to buy and sell cocaine.

608
00:38:50,600 --> 00:38:52,113
Is that correct?

609
00:38:53,680 --> 00:38:54,954
''On the advice of my attorney,

610
00:38:55,120 --> 00:38:57,953
I regretfully and respectfully
decline to answer

611
00:38:58,120 --> 00:38:59,599
on the constitutional grounds,

612
00:38:59,760 --> 00:39:02,320
protecting my rights
against self-incrimination.''

613
00:39:02,480 --> 00:39:03,879
Sir,

614
00:39:04,040 --> 00:39:08,511
at this time, I'd like to enter
into evidence these bank records.

615
00:39:09,480 --> 00:39:12,756
Maybe you can answer
this question for us, captain.

616
00:39:13,160 --> 00:39:15,833
This is a copy of a statement
from one of your savings accounts.

617
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:18,150
- Is that correct?
- Yes, sir.

618
00:39:18,320 --> 00:39:21,756
And on January 1 2th,
you deposited 1 5,000 dollars.

619
00:39:21,920 --> 00:39:22,909
Is that correct?

620
00:39:24,240 --> 00:39:26,834
- Yes, sir.
- Where did the money come from?

621
00:39:29,640 --> 00:39:32,154
''On the advice of my attorney,
I regretfully and respectfully

622
00:39:32,320 --> 00:39:34,709
- decline to answer on--''
- We get the picture.

623
00:39:34,880 --> 00:39:36,518
Sir,

624
00:39:37,320 --> 00:39:42,599
at this time, I'd like to enter
into evidence the bank records

625
00:39:42,760 --> 00:39:47,231
of Major Hamilton, Lieutenant Lynch,
and Colonel McCrea.

626
00:39:47,720 --> 00:39:50,678
As you can see, over the past six
months, each of these three men

627
00:39:50,840 --> 00:39:54,549
have deposited exactly
300,000 dollars.

628
00:39:54,720 --> 00:39:59,157
I call your attention now to the deposits
made on January 1 2th.

629
00:39:59,320 --> 00:40:03,029
Each of the other three conspirators
deposited 7,500 dollars.

630
00:40:05,080 --> 00:40:08,789
You deposited 1 5,000 dollars.

631
00:40:09,640 --> 00:40:11,437
Why did you get twice as much?

632
00:40:12,280 --> 00:40:14,271
I decline to answer.

633
00:40:14,440 --> 00:40:15,634
You know what I think?

634
00:40:15,800 --> 00:40:19,031
I think you got 7,500,
just like the others,

635
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:21,953
but then you took
Lieutenant Walters' share as well

636
00:40:22,120 --> 00:40:23,553
because you're the one
who killed him.

637
00:40:23,720 --> 00:40:26,951
Objection.
Counsel is arguing the evidence.

638
00:40:27,120 --> 00:40:28,109
Sustained.

639
00:40:28,280 --> 00:40:30,748
Just examine the witness,
please, Mr. Matlock.

640
00:40:30,920 --> 00:40:33,753
Yes, sir. You smoke a cigar,
don't you, captain?

641
00:40:33,920 --> 00:40:35,148
Yes.

642
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:38,309
You smoked one that night
in the back room, didn't you?

643
00:40:38,480 --> 00:40:39,549
I don't know. Probably.

644
00:40:39,720 --> 00:40:44,794
And you smoked another one
at the bar after everyone left.

645
00:40:45,520 --> 00:40:47,636
I don't think so, Mr. Matlock.

646
00:40:48,320 --> 00:40:52,108
You smoked a cigar
while you waited for Major Hamilton

647
00:40:52,280 --> 00:40:55,317
to return with a certain bayonet,

648
00:40:55,480 --> 00:40:59,917
and you left the butt of that cigar
in an ashtray on the bar, didn't you?

649
00:41:00,080 --> 00:41:01,399
No.

650
00:41:01,560 --> 00:41:03,949
Once you had the bayonet,

651
00:41:04,400 --> 00:41:08,188
you went back into that room,
where Lieutenant Walters

652
00:41:08,360 --> 00:41:10,191
was finishing his bookkeeping.

653
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:14,077
You went into that room
and you killed him.

654
00:41:15,040 --> 00:41:19,033
You stabbed Lieutenant Walters
to death with that bayonet, didn't you?

655
00:41:19,520 --> 00:41:21,317
None of that is true.

656
00:41:21,480 --> 00:41:23,072
You know,

657
00:41:23,320 --> 00:41:27,836
there are a couple of things
about this case that are really puzzling.

658
00:41:29,040 --> 00:41:31,873
I mean, the coroner said

659
00:41:32,040 --> 00:41:34,998
that Lieutenant Walters
had a raw abrasion on his hand

660
00:41:35,160 --> 00:41:38,232
that was not caused by the bayonet.
How do you suppose he got that?

661
00:41:39,360 --> 00:41:41,191
I haven't the faintest idea.

662
00:41:41,360 --> 00:41:44,033
And the blood on the seat
of the overturned chair

663
00:41:44,200 --> 00:41:48,751
indicates that Lieutenant Walters
was sitting with his back to the door

664
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:51,480
when he was stabbed.
He should've fallen forward,

665
00:41:51,640 --> 00:41:53,790
but his body was found
on the floor facing the door.

666
00:41:53,960 --> 00:41:55,393
How do you suppose
that happened?

667
00:41:56,760 --> 00:41:59,035
- I haven't the faintest idea.
- I do.

668
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:02,230
See, I think Lieutenant Walters

669
00:42:02,400 --> 00:42:05,119
was stabbed in a seated position,

670
00:42:05,800 --> 00:42:08,633
and as his killer turned to leave,

671
00:42:08,800 --> 00:42:12,952
with his last breath,
Lieutenant Walters rose up

672
00:42:13,120 --> 00:42:15,714
and grabbed the man from behind.

673
00:42:15,880 --> 00:42:17,074
You know what he grabbed?

674
00:42:17,800 --> 00:42:19,153
I haven't the faintest idea.

675
00:42:19,320 --> 00:42:21,595
The thing that cut his hand.

676
00:42:21,760 --> 00:42:22,749
The chain.

677
00:42:22,920 --> 00:42:25,309
The chain that holds the dog tags.

678
00:42:25,480 --> 00:42:29,837
Isn't it true that on January 1 4th,

679
00:42:30,000 --> 00:42:32,389
you had your wife

680
00:42:32,560 --> 00:42:35,757
take a gold chain
to a local jeweller for repairs?

681
00:42:38,080 --> 00:42:39,877
According to this work order,

682
00:42:41,400 --> 00:42:46,713
this gold chain
that ordinarily holds your dog tags.

683
00:42:46,880 --> 00:42:48,233
I had a chain fixed.

684
00:42:48,400 --> 00:42:50,595
- What does that prove?
- Nothing.

685
00:42:51,960 --> 00:42:54,474
But if Lieutenant Walters

686
00:42:54,840 --> 00:42:58,674
pulled this chain so hard
that he broke it and cut his hand,

687
00:42:58,840 --> 00:43:02,674
I'll bet you've got a pretty good cut
on your neck as well.

688
00:43:04,360 --> 00:43:06,590
You mind opening your shirt for us?

689
00:43:06,760 --> 00:43:08,432
Yes.

690
00:43:09,720 --> 00:43:12,029
Yes, yes, I do mind.

691
00:43:12,200 --> 00:43:14,760
You can open your shirt here,

692
00:43:15,520 --> 00:43:18,910
or the MPs will open it for you
back at the stockade.

693
00:43:33,520 --> 00:43:35,112
We...

694
00:43:36,480 --> 00:43:38,869
We drew straws for it.

695
00:43:39,040 --> 00:43:42,396
Colonel McCrea and Major Hamilton
are just as guilty as I am.

696
00:43:44,800 --> 00:43:47,917
Sir, in light of this new evidence
and testimony,

697
00:43:48,080 --> 00:43:50,196
defence moves for a dismissal.

698
00:43:51,040 --> 00:43:53,235
The court looks favourable
on your motion.

699
00:43:53,400 --> 00:43:55,834
Major Orlando, any objections?

700
00:43:56,040 --> 00:43:58,235
No, sir. No objections.

701
00:44:01,000 --> 00:44:03,719
The charges against Private Hughes
are dismissed.

702
00:44:12,200 --> 00:44:13,315
Thanks.

703
00:44:15,120 --> 00:44:16,838
You came through for me.

704
00:44:17,000 --> 00:44:18,513
Why don't you
give your father a call?

705
00:44:19,520 --> 00:44:20,669
Why would I do that?

706
00:44:20,840 --> 00:44:22,796
Show him the Army
made a man out of you.

707
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:24,757
Might set a good example for him.

708
00:44:32,640 --> 00:44:34,232
Thank you.

709
00:44:37,560 --> 00:44:39,551
Thank you, Charlene.

710
00:44:40,520 --> 00:44:43,318
Well, I guess
I don't have to ask who won.

711
00:44:43,480 --> 00:44:47,553
Tyler, there's an old tradition
we jailhouse lawyers like to practise

712
00:44:47,720 --> 00:44:50,473
after winning
a particularly difficult case.

713
00:44:50,640 --> 00:44:53,598
- There is?
- Oh, yeah.

714
00:44:53,760 --> 00:44:56,877
Give me five!

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
